---Linear-system method used by the spline least-squares fits.
---
---Every fit forms symmetric normal equations (B^T B + P) c = B^T y. Cholesky is exact but
---costs O(K^3) for K unknowns; conjugate gradient on the sparse system costs roughly
---O(nnz) per iteration and suits large, well-conditioned (usually penalized) systems.
---
---**Example**
---
---```cpp
---dm::fda::SplineSolveOptions options{dm::fda::SplineSolver::ConjugateGradient};
---```
---
---See also: `SplineSolveOptions`
---
---Arguments of this type also accept the enumerator names as strings.
---@enum datamunge.SplineSolver
datamunge.SplineSolver = {
    Automatic = 0,
    Cholesky = 1,
    ConjugateGradient = 2,
}

---Select an endpoint value or an outward-conormal natural boundary condition.
---The conormal p*du/dn uses -u' at the left and +u' at the right. Physical
---diffusive outward flux is its negative; these are different sign conventions.
---
---**Example**
---
---```cpp
---std::print("{}\n",dm::fem::BCType::Neumann); // Neumann
---```
---
---See also: `BoundaryCondition1D`
---
---Arguments of this type also accept the enumerator names as strings.
---@enum datamunge.BCType
datamunge.BCType = {
    Dirichlet = 0,
    Neumann = 1,
    Robin = 2,
}

---Selected predecessor of an actual positive DTW cell.
---advance_a changes the first coordinate, advance_b the second. Finite ties
---prefer diagonal, then advance_a, then advance_b. Unrepresentable accumulated
---costs have no finite predecessor witness; successful traceback never uses them.
---
---**Example**
---
---```cpp
---const auto t=dm::geometry::dynamic_time_warping_trace({1},{1});
---std::print("{}\n",t.predecessors[0]==dm::geometry::DTWPredecessor::origin); // true
---```
---
---See also: `DTWTrace`
---
---Arguments of this type also accept the enumerator names as strings.
---@enum datamunge.DTWPredecessor
datamunge.DTWPredecessor = {
    origin = 0,
    diagonal = 1,
    advance_a = 2,
    advance_b = 3,
    unrepresentable = 4,
}

