---C++ class `datamunge::optim::CuckooSearchOptions`
---@class datamunge.CuckooSearchOptions
---@field population_size integer Number of nests (candidate solutions) in the population.
---@field discovery_rate number pa: fraction of the worst nests discovered/abandoned each iteration. Yang & Deb's original paper headlines 0.25, but this implementation's "replace a randomly chosen comparison nest" Levy-flight rule (faithful to the paper) homogenizes the population fast enough at pa=0.25 that convergence quality becomes sharply seed-dependent on smooth objectives (verified: only 6/30 seeds converged well at pa=0.25, population_size=25 on a simple 3D sphere). 0.4 -- still within the commonly-used literature range -- injects enough fresh diversity each iteration to fix this at the default population size (verified: 30/30 seeds).
---@field levy_beta number Levy flight stability index, in (0, 2]; 1.5 is the standard choice in the original paper.
---@field step_scale number alpha: overall Levy step-size multiplier.
---@field max_iterations integer
---@field tolerance number Stops after the best-ever value has improved by less than this for 20 consecutive iterations.
---@field seed integer
---@overload fun(): datamunge.CuckooSearchOptions
datamunge.CuckooSearchOptions = {}

---Cuckoo Search (Yang & Deb, 2009) on an ArbitraryFunction within a box constraint: a
---population of nests is perturbed by heavy-tailed Levy flights (via Mantegna's
---algorithm) and a fraction of the worst nests is abandoned and rebuilt each
---iteration, mimicking brood parasitism. Derivative-free.
---@class datamunge.CuckooSearch
---@overload fun(options?: datamunge.CuckooSearchOptions): datamunge.CuckooSearch
datamunge.CuckooSearch = {}

