---@param src datamunge.Image
---@param x number
---@param y number
---@return datamunge.Pixel
function datamunge.bilinear_sample(src, x, y) end

---A new image of size `new_width` x `new_height`, resampled from `src`. Nearest
---maps each destination pixel to its closest source pixel (blocky, but exact for
---integer scale factors); Bilinear interpolates the four surrounding source pixels
---(smoother, the usual default for photographic content).
---@param src datamunge.Image
---@param new_width integer
---@param new_height integer
---@param filter? datamunge.ResampleFilter (default: `datamunge.ResampleFilter.Bilinear`)
---@return datamunge.Image
function datamunge.resize(src, new_width, new_height, filter) end

---The sub-image [x, x+width) x [y, y+height) of `src`. Throws std::out_of_range if
---the requested rectangle isn't fully contained in `src`.
---@param src datamunge.Image
---@param x integer
---@param y integer
---@param width integer
---@param height integer
---@return datamunge.Image
function datamunge.crop(src, x, y, width, height) end

---A new image with every row reversed left-to-right (mirror image).
---@param src datamunge.Image
---@return datamunge.Image
function datamunge.flip_horizontal(src) end

---A new image with every column reversed top-to-bottom (upside down).
---@param src datamunge.Image
---@return datamunge.Image
function datamunge.flip_vertical(src) end

---A new image rotated clockwise by exactly 90, 180, or 270 degrees (any other value
---throws std::invalid_argument -- use rotate() for arbitrary angles). 90/270 swap
---width and height; 180 keeps them.
---@param src datamunge.Image
---@param degrees integer
---@return datamunge.Image
function datamunge.rotate90(src, degrees) end

---A new image rotated clockwise by an arbitrary angle (in degrees) about its center,
---via inverse-mapping + bilinear resampling. The output canvas is exactly large enough
---to contain the fully-rotated source (like Pillow's expand=True), and pixels sourced
---from outside the original image are filled with `fill_color`.
---@param src datamunge.Image
---@param degrees number
---@param fill_color? datamunge.Pixel (default: `datamunge.Pixel()`)
---@return datamunge.Image
function datamunge.rotate(src, degrees, fill_color) end

---The classical Gaussian pyramid: level 0 is `img` itself; each subsequent level is
---the previous level Gaussian-blurred (sigma = `sigma`) then downsampled by 2x
---(bilinear resize) -- a coarse-to-fine multi-resolution representation used
---throughout this module (e.g. as the natural foundation for a pyramidal optical-flow
---extension, though lucas_kanade_optical_flow() itself stays single-level). Stops
---early (producing fewer than `levels` images) if a level's dimensions would shrink
---below 1x1. Throws std::invalid_argument for levels < 1.
---@param img datamunge.Image
---@param levels integer
---@param sigma? number (default: `1.0`)
---@return datamunge.Image[]
function datamunge.gaussian_pyramid(img, levels, sigma) end

---Per-channel signed difference (cur - upsampled), re-centered at 128 and clamped to
---[0, 255] so it stores in an ordinary Image -- the standard way to make a Laplacian
---pyramid level (which is naturally signed) visualizable/storable without introducing
---a separate signed-pixel image type just for this.
---@param cur datamunge.Image
---@param upsampled datamunge.Image
---@return datamunge.Image
function datamunge.signed_difference_image(cur, upsampled) end

---The Laplacian pyramid: for every level except the last, level_i = (Gaussian level i)
---minus (Gaussian level i+1 upsampled back to level i's size) -- the detail lost by
---blurring+downsampling at that scale, re-centered at 128 (see
---detail::signed_difference_image()) so it stores as an ordinary image; the final
---level is the smallest Gaussian level kept as-is (the residual low-frequency image
---the whole pyramid was built from). Reconstructing the original from a Laplacian
---pyramid is the classical use (progressively add each un-recentered level back onto
---the upsampled reconstruction-so-far) but isn't implemented here -- this module only
---needed the decomposition direction.
---@param img datamunge.Image
---@param levels integer
---@param sigma? number (default: `1.0`)
---@return datamunge.Image[]
function datamunge.laplacian_pyramid(img, levels, sigma) end

---Labels every foreground (luma >= 128) connected region of `img` with a distinct
---positive integer via flood fill (simple breadth-first flood fill per unvisited
---foreground pixel, rather than the classical two-pass union-find labeling algorithm
----- same asymptotic complexity, much easier to verify correct, matching this module's
---established simplicity-over-cleverness bias). `eight_connected` selects 8-
---neighborhood (diagonals count) vs. 4-neighborhood (orthogonal only) connectivity.
---@param img datamunge.Image
---@param eight_connected? boolean (default: `true`)
---@return datamunge.LabelMap
function datamunge.connected_components(img, eight_connected) end

---Otsu's method: the grayscale threshold (0-255) that maximizes the between-class
---variance of the pixels it would split into "below" and "at-or-above" groups --
---the standard automatic threshold selection when a fixed threshold isn't known in
---advance. Pairs directly with image::threshold(img, otsu_threshold(img)).
---@param img datamunge.Image
---@return integer
function datamunge.otsu_threshold(img) end

---K-means color segmentation: clusters every pixel of `img` by its RGB value into `k`
---clusters (Lloyd's algorithm, deterministic fixed-seed initialization by sampling k
---distinct pixels) and returns a new RGB image where every pixel is replaced by its
---assigned cluster's mean color. Throws std::invalid_argument if `k` exceeds the
---number of distinct pixels available to seed from.
---@param img datamunge.Image
---@param k integer
---@param max_iterations? integer (default: `20`)
---@param seed? integer (default: `42`)
---@return datamunge.Image
function datamunge.kmeans_segment(img, k, max_iterations, seed) end

---The cache root where downloaded datasets are stored.
---
---The first of: `$DATAMUNGE_DATA_DIR`; `$XDG_CACHE_HOME/datamunge/datasets`;
---`$HOME/.cache/datamunge/datasets`; on Windows `%LOCALAPPDATA%/datamunge/datasets`;
---otherwise `build/datasets` relative to the working directory. Nothing is created.
---
---**Example**
---
---```cpp
---std::print("{}\n", dm::datasets::data_home());   // e.g. /home/me/.cache/datamunge/datasets
---```
---
---See also: `fetch()`
---@return string # The directory path (it may not exist yet).
function datamunge.data_home() end

---The names fetch() accepts, sorted: "california_housing", "cifar10", "cifar100",
---"diamonds", "fashion_mnist", "karate_club", "mnist", "rebot_devarm", "sudoku".
---
---**Example**
---
---```cpp
---for (const auto& name : dm::datasets::downloadable()) std::print("{}\n", name);
---```
---@return string[]
function datamunge.downloadable() end

---Makes sure a downloadable dataset is on disk and returns where it is.
---
---For the tabular datasets ("diamonds", "california_housing") this is the CSV file. For the
---image datasets it is a directory: "mnist" and "fashion_mnist" hold the four uncompressed
---IDX files (`train-images-idx3-ubyte`, ...); "cifar10" holds `data_batch_1..5.bin` and
---`test_batch.bin`; "cifar100" holds `train.bin` and `test.bin` (the binary versions);
---"sudoku" holds Norvig's `easy50.txt`, `top95.txt` and `hardest.txt`.
---"karate_club" holds the970-byte original ZIP, GML topology and source README;
---every cached archive/member is checksum-verified. karate_club() reads34nodes/78edges.
---"rebot_devarm" holds 38 pinned Seeed DevArm CAD/URDF/STL files and the
---CERN-OHL-W-2.0 source license in their upstream relative directory layout.
---DevArm verifies the size and SHA-256 of every cached file on every call;
---changed files throw without replacement. Missing files are staged and verified
---before publication. It uses the same offline/mirror/cache controls, and works
---without the optional native data-preparation dependencies. Hashing uses memory
---proportional to the largest source file (about 35 MB).
---Approximate download sizes: diamonds 2.7 MB, california_housing 1.4 MB, MNIST 11 MB,
---Fashion-MNIST 30 MB, CIFAR-10 163 MB, CIFAR-100 161 MB, sudoku 14 KB, DevArm 56 MB.
---
---**Example**
---
---```cpp
---const auto housing = dm::datasets::read_csv(dm::datasets::fetch("california_housing"));
---```
---
---For a complete CAD asset tree, use `dm::datasets::fetch("rebot_devarm")`.
---
---**References**
---
---- Seeed reBot-DevArm source: https://github.com/Seeed-Projects/reBot-DevArm
---
---**Errors** (raised as Lua errors)
---* `std::invalid_argument`: for an unknown name.
---* `std::runtime_error`: if files are missing and downloading is disabled or fails, or a download does not match its checksum.
---
---See also: `data_home()`, `downloadable()`, `download_file()`, `karate_club()`
---@param name string One of downloadable().
---@param directory? string Where the files live; empty means `data_home() + "/" + name`. Files already present there are used as they are, except DevArm and karate_club's verified files. (default: `""`)
---@return string # The local path of the CSV file or the dataset directory.
function datamunge.fetch(name, directory) end

---Downloads `url` to `destination` with curl, unless the destination already exists.
---
---The file is downloaded into a temporary directory next to `destination`, checked, and only
---then renamed into place. Parent directories are created as needed. `DATAMUNGE_DATA_OFFLINE`
---is honored; `DATAMUNGE_DATA_MIRROR` is not (fetch() applies it).
---
---**Example**
---
---```cpp
---dm::datasets::download_file("https://example.org/data.csv", "/tmp/data.csv");
---```
---
---**Errors** (raised as Lua errors)
---* `std::runtime_error`: if downloading is disabled or fails, or the checksum differs.
---@param url string An `https://`, `http://` or `file://` URL.
---@param destination string The final file path.
---@param md5? string Expected lowercase hex MD5 of the downloaded bytes; empty skips the check. (default: `""`)
function datamunge.download_file(url, destination, md5) end

---Builds a uniform mesh of `num_elements` equal-length elements on [a, b].
---Throws std::invalid_argument if num_elements is 0 or b <= a.
---@param a number
---@param b number
---@param num_elements integer
---@return datamunge.Mesh1D
function datamunge.make_uniform_mesh1d(a, b, num_elements) end

---Evaluate squared Euclidean distance using the original double arithmetic.
---No finite/range validation. Differences, products and sum can overflow,
---underflow or lose low bits; NaNs propagate according to floating arithmetic.
---
---**Example**
---
---```cpp
---std::cout << dm::geometry::squared_distance({0,0},{3,4}); // 25
---```
---
---See also: `distance()`
---@param a datamunge.Point2D First point.
---@param b datamunge.Point2D Second point.
---@return number # Rounded squared distance, possibly infinity/NaN or underflowed zero.
function datamunge.squared_distance(a, b) end

---Return sqrt(squared_distance(a,b)) with its original range limitations.
---A finite mathematical distance may overflow through the squared intermediate;
---this convenience primitive performs no checked hypot or input validation.
---
---**Example**
---
---```cpp
---std::cout << dm::geometry::distance({0,0},{3,4}); // 5
---```
---
---See also: `squared_distance()`
---@param a datamunge.Point2D First point.
---@param b datamunge.Point2D Second point.
---@return number # Rounded square root of the double-valued squared distance.
function datamunge.distance(a, b) end

---Evaluate the original double determinant (b-a) cross (c-a).
---A positive exact determinant means counterclockwise, but this approximation
---can cancel, overflow or underflow and its returned sign is not certified.
---No tolerance or finite validation is applied. orientation_sign() supplies an
---exact sign for finite stored binary64 points; this primitive remains unchanged.
---
---**Example**
---
---```cpp
---std::cout << dm::geometry::cross({0,0},{1,0},{0,1}); // 1
---```
---
---See also: `orientation_sign()`
---@param a datamunge.Point2D First point.
---@param b datamunge.Point2D Second point.
---@param c datamunge.Point2D Third point.
---@return number # Rounded double determinant, possibly infinity/NaN or a misleading zero.
function datamunge.cross(a, b, c) end

---Construct a complete unconstrained Delaunay triangulation with exact signs.
---Retains the original one-vector signature and Triangle layout. Finite coordinate
---validation precedes even trivial results. Duplicates coalesce to earliest ids;
---collinear/fewer-than-three distinct sites return no faces. Uses Bowyer-Watson
---cavity replacement with symbolic hull ghosts, avoiding any finite super-triangle
---whose proximity could remove valid narrow faces. All orientation/incircle signs
---are exact for finite stored binary64, including subnormals/extreme scales.
---Cocircular ties retain existing faces under the ordering in DelaunayTrace.
---Faces are strictly CCW, contain no ghosts and reference original ids. Finite
---output order is canonical as documented on DelaunayTrace. No boundary constraints,
---holes, Steiner refinement or minimum-angle guarantee is supplied. Input unchanged;
---O(n log(n+1)+u^2 log(u+1)) worst-case work for n records/u distinct sites with
---ordered cavity maps and full live scans; O(n+u) working/output storage, no shared
---state or optional resource caps. Exact-predicate cost is bounded per binary64 query.
---
---**Example**
---
---```cpp
---std::print("{}\n",dm::geometry::delaunay_triangulation({{0,0},{1,0},{.5,.000001}}).size()); // 1
---```
---
---**References**
---
---- A. Bowyer (1981), Computing Dirichlet tessellations, Computer Journal 24(2),162–166, doi:10.1093/comjnl/24.2.162.
---- D. F. Watson (1981), Computing the n-dimensional Delaunay tessellation with application to Voronoi polytopes, Computer Journal 24(2),167–172, doi:10.1093/comjnl/24.2.167. This variant uses symbolic boundary faces, exact decoded-dyadic signs, earliest duplicate identities and ordered cavity edges.
---
---**Errors** (raised as Lua errors)
---* `std::invalid_argument`: Any nonfinite coordinate, even on short input.
---* `std::length_error`: Counter arithmetic or container capacity exceeded.
---* `std::logic_error`: Internal cavity topology invariant fails; no partial mesh returned.
---* `std::bad_alloc`: Allocation fails.
---
---See also: `delaunay_trace()`, `incircle_sign()`
---@param points datamunge.Point2D[] Borrowed finite planar sites, possibly empty/duplicate/collinear.
---@return datamunge.Triangle[] # Owned complete finite mesh of the distinct sites' convex hull, or no faces for collinearity.
function datamunge.delaunay_triangulation(points) end

---Capture actual symbolic-boundary cavity transitions from the same solver.
---No replay. Full finite validation precedes the original-record cap and all
---allocation. max_tests bounds cumulative incircle plus hull-side calls, checked
---before each complete live-face scan. Trace entries charge one per insertion
---plus removed faces, boundary edges and added faces, including ghosts, checked
---before retaining the event. Points/maps/final output and temporary cavities are
---excluded from that entry count; caps do not bound bytes or wall time. Zero
---disables only an optional cap. O(sum cavity/event sizes) additional history,
---at worst O(u^2), beyond the ordinary solver. Failure returns no partial result.
---
---**Example**
---
---```cpp
---const auto t=dm::geometry::delaunay_trace({{0,0},{4,0},{4,4},{0,4},{2,2}},5,10);
---std::print("{} {}\n",t.triangles.size(),t.work.incircle_tests+t.work.hull_side_tests); // 4 10
---```
---
---**References**
---
---- Bowyer (1981), doi:10.1093/comjnl/24.2.162; Watson (1981), doi:10.1093/comjnl/24.2.167; records the symbolic-boundary variant rather than a geometric replay.
---
---**Errors** (raised as Lua errors)
---* `std::invalid_argument`: Any nonfinite coordinate.
---* `std::length_error`: Optional cap, count arithmetic or container capacity exceeded.
---* `std::logic_error`: Internal cavity invariant fails.
---* `std::bad_alloc`: Allocation fails.
---
---See also: `DelaunayTrace`, `delaunay_triangulation()`
---@param points datamunge.Point2D[] Finite original coordinates, unchanged.
---@param max_points? integer Complete original-record cap, default 1000000; zero disables. (default: `1000000`)
---@param max_tests? integer Cumulative complete-scan conflict cap, default 10000000; zero disables. (default: `10000000`)
---@param max_entries? integer Cumulative event-entry cap, default 1000000; zero disables. (default: `1000000`)
---@return datamunge.DelaunayTrace # Owned points, original-id maps, finite mesh, actual events and work.
function datamunge.delaunay_trace(points, max_points, max_tests, max_entries) end

---Measure a supplied mesh and test every nonvertex site against every circle.
---Performs the structural checks on DelaunayAnalysis first. The complete prospective
---circle count faces*(unique_sites-3) is checked before circle queries; zero cap
---disables only that limit. O(n log(n+1)+f log(f+1)+fu) work and O(n+f) storage;
---minimum angles are floating diagnostics and are not certified bounds. This is a
---local property analysis, not a global embedding/coverage certificate.
---
---**Example**
---
---```cpp
---const std::vector<dm::geometry::Point2D> p{{0,0},{1,0},{1,1},{0,1}};
---const auto a=dm::geometry::delaunay_analyze(p,dm::geometry::delaunay_triangulation(p));
---std::print("{} {} {}\n",a.edges,a.boundary_edges,a.circle_tests); // 5 4 2
---```
---
---**Errors** (raised as Lua errors)
---* `std::invalid_argument`: Nonfinite coordinates, bad/degenerate/non-CCW/duplicate faces or nonmanifold edges.
---* `std::length_error`: Circle-count arithmetic or optional cap exceeded.
---* `std::bad_alloc`: Allocation fails.
---
---See also: `DelaunayAnalysis`, `delaunay_triangulation()`
---@param points datamunge.Point2D[] Finite original sites, unchanged.
---@param triangles datamunge.Triangle[] Finite CCW faces with valid original indices, unchanged.
---@param max_tests? integer Complete exact-circle query cap, default 1000000; zero disables. (default: `1000000`)
---@return datamunge.DelaunayAnalysis # Owned scalar topology, actual query/violation counts and approximate angle.
function datamunge.delaunay_analyze(points, triangles, max_tests) end

---Generate the report's seeded uniform square-cloud control in the core.
---Uses mt19937(seed) and uniform_real_distribution<double>(0,extent), x then y
---for each point. Same-library runs reproduce the original report's seed20250728
---and 30-site square when defaults are used. Standard distribution algorithms
---need not match across C++ libraries, so the seed is not a universal byte fixture.
---Coincident draws are allowed and later triangulation deduplicates them.
---O(count) work/storage, owned output, no global RNG or shared mutation.
---
---**Example**
---
---```cpp
---std::print("{}\n",dm::geometry::delaunay_uniform_cloud(30).size()); // 30
---```
---
---**Errors** (raised as Lua errors)
---* `std::invalid_argument`: Nonpositive or nonfinite extent.
---* `std::length_error`: Optional count cap or container capacity exceeded before allocation.
---* `std::bad_alloc`: Allocation fails.
---
---See also: `delaunay_trace()`
---@param count integer Number of original generated records, zero allowed.
---@param seed? integer mt19937 seed, default 20250728. (default: `20250728`)
---@param extent? number Positive finite square extent, default 10; checked even for count zero. (default: `10.0`)
---@param max_points? integer Optional complete output-record cap, default 1000000; zero disables. (default: `1000000`)
---@return datamunge.Point2D[] # Owned generated points in [0,extent) on each axis.
function datamunge.delaunay_uniform_cloud(count, seed, extent, max_points) end

---Builds a structured rectangular mesh on [x0, x1] x [y0, y1]: nx * ny grid cells,
---each split into two counterclockwise triangles, for a total of nx*ny*2 triangles and
---(nx+1)*(ny+1) nodes, ordered row-major (x fastest, then y).
---Throws std::invalid_argument if nx or ny is 0, or if x1 <= x0 or y1 <= y0.
---@param x0 number
---@param y0 number
---@param x1 number
---@param y1 number
---@param nx integer
---@param ny integer
---@return datamunge.Mesh2D
function datamunge.make_rectangular_mesh2d(x0, y0, x1, y1, nx, ny) end

---Builds a mesh over the convex hull of an arbitrary point cloud via Delaunay
---triangulation (datamunge::geometry::delaunay_triangulation) -- useful for
---unstructured domains a structured grid does not fit.
---@param points datamunge.Point2D[]
---@return datamunge.Mesh2D
function datamunge.make_mesh2d_from_points(points) end

---Node indices lying on the mesh's outer boundary: every node incident to a boundary
---edge (an edge belonging to exactly one triangle). Works generically for both a
---structured rectangular mesh (the domain's four sides) and an unstructured Delaunay
---mesh (the convex hull), returned in ascending order with no duplicates.
---@param mesh datamunge.Mesh2D
---@return integer[]
function datamunge.boundary_nodes(mesh) end

---The axis-aligned bounding box of `points`. Throws std::invalid_argument for an
---empty point set.
---@param points datamunge.Point2D[]
---@return datamunge.BoundingBox
function datamunge.bounding_box(points) end

---Return the closest point copies and finite-double distance using the checked all-pairs kernel.
---Validates at least two finite points, visits every original i<j pair and compares
---widened hypot distances. Equal rounded distances keep the lexicographically
---first index pair. Duplicates are valid distinct records and can yield zero.
---No early stop, sorting or spatial pruning; O(n^2) work and O(1) auxiliary storage.
---No input mutation/shared state. Widened comparison can change former false
---zero/infinite results and last-bit ties; it is not an exact-real predicate.
---The old call/result layout remains; indexed/traced variants retain input ids.
---
---**Example**
---
---```cpp
---const auto r=dm::geometry::closest_pair({{0,0},{3,4},{0,1}});
---std::print("{}\n",r.distance); // 1
---```
---
---**Errors** (raised as Lua errors)
---* `std::invalid_argument`: If point count is too small or any coordinate is nonfinite.
---* `std::length_error`: If complete pair count cannot fit size_t.
---* `std::overflow_error`: If arithmetic/counters or the selected legacy distance cannot fit.
---
---See also: `closest_pair_indexed()`, `closest_pair_trace()`
---@param points datamunge.Point2D[] Borrowed original finite planar coordinates, length at least two.
---@return datamunge.ClosestPairResult # Owned selected point copies and checked finite-double measured distance.
function datamunge.closest_pair(points) end

---Compute Andrew's strict monotone-chain hull using exact binary64 orientation signs.
---Validates all coordinates before internal indexed copies or sorting; the
---original by-value argument copy occurs at the call boundary. Exact coordinate
---equality removes duplicates (including signed zero); the earliest matching
---original record supplies its representation. Returns lexicographic-minimum
---first, counterclockwise, no repeated endpoint and no collinear edge interiors.
---Empty/singleton/two-point/collinear outputs follow ConvexHullAnalysis. O(n log n)
---comparisons plus O(n) bounded exact-sign tests, O(n) scratch/output, no global
---state. The legacy by-value signature is retained and has no optional caps.
---Other geometry routines that use cross() retain their own floating contracts.
---
---**Example**
---
---```cpp
---const auto h=dm::geometry::convex_hull({{0,0},{2,0},{2,2},{0,2},{1,1}});
---std::cout << h.size(); // 4
---```
---
---**References**
---
---- A. M. Andrew (1979), Another efficient algorithm for convex hulls in two
---  dimensions, https://doi.org/10.1016/0020-0190(79)90072-3 .
---
---**Errors** (raised as Lua errors)
---* `std::invalid_argument`: Any nonfinite coordinate.
---* `std::length_error`: Work/container size exhaustion.
---
---See also: `convex_hull_analyze()`, `convex_hull_trace()`, `orientation_sign()`
---@param points datamunge.Point2D[] Finite planar records, copied by value as in the original API.
---@return datamunge.Point2D[] # Owned strict hull of the represented coordinates.
function datamunge.convex_hull(points) end

---Execute the same exact-sign hull kernel with identities and actual work.
---Same ordering, degeneracy and complexity contract as convex_hull(). Full finite
---validation precedes the complete max_points cap and scratch allocation. No
---events or completed-chain snapshots are retained; event cap is ignored.
---
---**Example**
---
---```cpp
---const auto a=dm::geometry::convex_hull_analyze({{0,0},{1,0},{2,0}});
---std::cout << a.hull.size() << ' ' << a.work.distinct_points; // 2 3
---```
---
---**References**
---
---- Andrew (1979), https://doi.org/10.1016/0020-0190(79)90072-3 .
---
---**Errors** (raised as Lua errors)
---* `std::invalid_argument`: Nonfinite coordinate.
---* `std::length_error`: Complete point cap or work/container exhaustion.
---
---See also: `convex_hull()`, `ConvexHullOptions`
---@param points datamunge.Point2D[] Finite original records; not mutated.
---@param options? datamunge.ConvexHullOptions Complete original-record cap, zero unlimited; defaults one million. (default: `datamunge.ConvexHullOptions()`)
---@return datamunge.ConvexHullAnalysis # Owned hull, earliest original indices and actual counters.
function datamunge.convex_hull_analyze(points, options) end

---Capture the hull kernel's actual stack events without replay or quadratic snapshots.
---Same computation/work as analyze, plus O(n) owned sorted records, compact
---test/pop/push edits and completed lower/upper chains. Positive max_events is
---checked online on actual records, including pushes; zero disables the cap.
---
---**Example**
---
---```cpp
---const auto t=dm::geometry::convex_hull_trace({{0,0},{1,0}});
---std::cout << t.events.size(); // 4
---```
---
---**References**
---
---- Andrew (1979), https://doi.org/10.1016/0020-0190(79)90072-3 .
---
---**Errors** (raised as Lua errors)
---* `std::invalid_argument`: Nonfinite coordinate.
---* `std::length_error`: Point/event cap or work/container exhaustion.
---
---See also: `convex_hull_analyze()`, `HullEvent`
---@param points datamunge.Point2D[] Finite original records; not mutated.
---@param options? datamunge.ConvexHullOptions Complete point and actual event caps. (default: `datamunge.ConvexHullOptions()`)
---@return datamunge.ConvexHullTrace # Owned actual sorted coordinates, identities, work and scan edits.
function datamunge.convex_hull_trace(points, options) end

---Draw unsnapped independent standard-normal x,y pairs with a local 32-bit MT stream.
---Reproduces the original hull report's mt19937/normal_distribution call order,
---including its cached normal variate. Distribution mappings can differ between
---standard libraries; a seed alone does not promise portable coordinate bits.
---Complete count cap precedes allocation. Empty count is valid. O(count) expected
---work/storage; no global RNG or mutation. Finite draws are checked before return.
---
---**Example**
---
---```cpp
---std::cout << dm::geometry::sample_gaussian_points(40,20240728).size(); // 40
---```
---
---**References**
---
---- C++ normal_distribution specification: https://eel.is/c++draft/rand.dist.norm.normal .
---
---**Errors** (raised as Lua errors)
---* `std::length_error`: Complete draw cap/container exhaustion.
---* `std::overflow_error`: A generated coordinate is nonfinite.
---
---See also: `convex_hull()`, `random_point_cloud()`
---@param count? integer Number of points, default forty. (default: `40`)
---@param seed? integer Local mt19937 seed, default 20240728. (default: `20240728`)
---@param max_points? integer Complete draw cap, default one million; zero unlimited. (default: `1000000`)
---@return datamunge.Point2D[] # Owned unsnapped normal point cloud.
function datamunge.sample_gaussian_points(count, seed, max_points) end

---`n` colours from green through yellow and brown to near-white, as R's `terrain.colors(n)`.
---
---**Errors** (raised as Lua errors)
---* `std::invalid_argument`: If `n` is 0.
---@param n integer Number of colours (at least 1).
---@return datamunge.RGB[] # The palette, low to high.
function datamunge.terrain_colors(n) end

---`n` colours from dark blue through white to dark red, a diverging palette for signed
---values such as correlations (ColorBrewer's RdBu, reversed so that low is blue).
---
---Interpolates linearly between the seven RdBu anchor colours; an odd `n` puts pure
---near-white (247, 247, 247) in the middle, so with `z_min = -z_max` in RPlot::image() zero
---maps to white.
---
---**Example**
---
---```cpp
---const auto pal = dm::graphics::diverging_colors(3);
---std::print("{} {} {}\n", pal[0].r, pal[1].g, pal[2].r);  // 33 247 178
---```
---
---**References**
---
---- C. Brewer, ColorBrewer 2.0 (RdBu scheme): https://colorbrewer2.org
---
---**Errors** (raised as Lua errors)
---* `std::invalid_argument`: If `n` is 0.
---
---See also: `terrain_colors()`, `RPlot::image()`
---@param n integer Number of colours (at least 1; 1 gives the white midpoint).
---@return datamunge.RGB[] # The palette, low (blue) to high (red).
function datamunge.diverging_colors(n) end

---`n` colours of the viridis scale, from dark purple through blue and green to yellow,
---matching R's `viridisLite::viridis(n)` at n = 10.
---
---Viridis is perceptually uniform, so equal steps in value look like equal steps in colour,
---and it stays readable in greyscale and for the common forms of colour blindness. Colours are
---interpolated linearly between viridis(10)'s ten anchors.
---
---**Example**
---
---```cpp
---const auto pal = dm::graphics::viridis_colors(10);
---std::print("{} {} {}\n", pal[0].r, pal[5].g, pal[9].b);  // 68 158 37
---```
---
---**References**
---
---- S. van der Walt and N. Smith (2015). "mpl colourmaps" (viridis): https://bids.github.io/colormap/
---- viridisLite: https://sjmgarnier.github.io/viridisLite/
---
---**Errors** (raised as Lua errors)
---* `std::invalid_argument`: If `n` is 0.
---
---See also: `value_colors()`, `terrain_colors()`
---@param n integer Number of colours (at least 1).
---@return datamunge.RGB[] # The palette, low (purple) to high (yellow).
function datamunge.viridis_colors(n) end

---One colour per value on a continuous scale, for colouring scatter points by a
---numeric variable (ggplot2's `scale_colour_viridis_c()`).
---
---Each value is mapped linearly from [`z_min`, `z_max`] to the palette and rounded to the
---nearest entry; values outside are clamped, NaN values get grey (190, 190, 190), and a zero
---range maps everything to the middle colour. Pass the result to RPlot::points_colored().
---
---**Example**
---
---```cpp
---const auto cols = dm::graphics::value_colors({0.0, 0.5, 1.0}, dm::graphics::viridis_colors(3));
---std::print("{} {} {}\n", cols[0].r, cols[1].r, cols[2].r);  // 68 35 253
---```
---
---See also: `viridis_colors()`, `RPlot::points_colored()`
---@param values number[] Values to colour.
---@param palette? datamunge.RGB[] Colours from low to high (default viridis_colors(256)). (default: `{}`)
---@param z_min? number Value of the first colour; NaN (the default) uses the smallest value.
---@param z_max? number Value of the last colour; NaN (the default) uses the largest value.
---@return datamunge.RGB[] # One colour per value.
function datamunge.value_colors(values, palette, z_min, z_max) end

---Compute the complete logical capture size without allocating a DTW matrix.
---Checks positive lengths, int path-index representability and size_t geometry.
---A representable count does not promise that the corresponding buffers fit memory.
---
---**Example**
---
---```cpp
---std::print("{}\n",dm::geometry::dtw_trace_entries(3,4)); // 31
---```
---
---**Errors** (raised as Lua errors)
---* `std::invalid_argument`: For either zero length.
---* `std::length_error`: For index, product or capture-count overflow.
---
---See also: `dynamic_time_warping_trace()`
---@param n integer Positive first length, at most INT_MAX.
---@param m integer Positive second length, at most INT_MAX.
---@return integer # n+m+2*n*m logical entries, excluding result paths and scratch rows.
function datamunge.dtw_trace_entries(n, m) end

---Compute unrestricted absolute-loss DTW and actual logical work without cost history.
---Owns the result; borrows finite inputs without mutation. No band, slope bound,
---step weighting, length normalization or fitted parameters. Finite ties choose
---diagonal, then advance_a, then advance_b. O(n*m) recurrence time, O(n*m+n+m)
---scratch/output, with only two double cost rows and n*m predecessor bytes.
---Complete input/geometry validation precedes allocation. Positive overflowing
---off-path costs remain infinity; only a nonfinite final optimum is rejected.
---Under ordinary double arithmetic the returned witness sums to the returned
---distance in forward order. Concurrent independent calls share no mutable state.
---
---**Example**
---
---See DTWWork and DTWComputation for executed work examples.
---
---**References**
---
---- H. Sakoe and S. Chiba (1978), Dynamic programming algorithm optimization for
---  spoken word recognition, IEEE TASSP26(1):43-49. doi:10.1109/TASSP.1978.1163055.
---  This is the unrestricted scalar absolute-loss variant, without the paper's window/slope rules.
---
---**Errors** (raised as Lua errors)
---* `std::invalid_argument`: For empty or nonfinite input.
---* `std::length_error`: For geometry/index/container bounds.
---* `std::overflow_error`: If the final minimum is not finite double.
---* `std::bad_alloc`: If scratch or result storage cannot be allocated.
---
---See also: `dynamic_time_warping()`, `dynamic_time_warping_trace()`
---@param a number[] Nonempty finite scalar sequence, length at most INT_MAX.
---@param b number[] Nonempty finite scalar sequence, length at most INT_MAX.
---@return datamunge.DTWComputation # Independent optimal result and counts, with no retained input/cost history.
function datamunge.dynamic_time_warping_work(a, b) end

---Preserve the original two-argument unrestricted absolute-loss DTW entry point.
---Same validation, ownership, deterministic ties, numerical limits and complexity
---as dynamic_time_warping_work(); discards only its work counters. This distance
---is not a metric: unequal stretched sequences can have zero cost and the triangle
---inequality can fail. Equal-length diagonal alignment bounds it by rigid L1 loss.
---
---**Example**
---
---See DTWResult for the executed original stretched-sequence example.
---
---**References**
---
---- H. Sakoe and S. Chiba (1978), IEEE TASSP26(1):43-49, doi:10.1109/TASSP.1978.1163055.
---
---**Errors** (raised as Lua errors)
---* `std::invalid_argument`: For empty or nonfinite input.
---* `std::length_error`: For geometry/index/container bounds.
---* `std::overflow_error`: If the final optimum is not finite double.
---* `std::bad_alloc`: If scratch or result storage cannot be allocated.
---
---See also: `dynamic_time_warping_work()`, `dtw_path_cost()`
---@param a number[] Nonempty finite first sequence, length at most INT_MAX.
---@param b number[] Nonempty finite second sequence, length at most INT_MAX.
---@return datamunge.DTWResult # Owned optimal distance and inclusive zero-based parallel int paths.
function datamunge.dynamic_time_warping(a, b) end

---Capture actual positive-cell costs and predecessor choices from the shared DTW kernel.
---Validates complete finite inputs/geometry before checking the optional complete
---capture budget, then copies inputs and runs once. Owns every snapshot. No partial
---trace/result escapes on failure. Same variant/ties/numerical rules as ordinary DTW.
---
---**Example**
---
---See DTWTrace and DTWPredecessor for executed capture examples.
---
---**References**
---
---- H. Sakoe and S. Chiba (1978), IEEE TASSP26(1):43-49, doi:10.1109/TASSP.1978.1163055.
